course: geography
lesson: 19
title: Straits: Bosporus, Gibraltar, Malacca, Hormuz and the other narrow doors
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A strait is a narrow stretch of sea joining two larger bodies of water, like a door between two rooms. Ships that want to go from one room to the other must squeeze through the door, so whoever holds its sides can watch, tax or block them.
explain: That is why straits made rulers rich and fought over: Constantinople guarded the Bosporus, Denmark charged every foreign ship a toll at the Øresund from 1429 to 1857, and Portugal seized Malacca in 1511 and Hormuz in 1515.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5, straits are places where armies can cross between lands and where forts and trade are valuable, and that is true. But real straits are not all alike: Gibraltar is about 14 km wide while the Drake Passage is about 800 km of some of the roughest sea on Earth.
explain: Today straits still matter: about a fifth of the oil the world uses normally passes through the Strait of Hormuz, so trouble there can make petrol dearer far away.
explain: Think of a school corridor with one narrow door at the end: when one person stops in it, the whole queue gets stuck behind.
fact: A strait is a narrow stretch of sea joining two larger bodies of water, and a few straits carry a large share of the world's shipping.
fact: The Bosporus joins the Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara, is less than 1 km wide at its narrowest, and divides Istanbul between Europe and Asia.
fact: The Dardanelles links the Sea of Marmara to the Aegean, a part of the Mediterranean, and with the Bosporus it forms the Turkish Straits, the only sea way from the Black Sea to the Mediterranean, whose use by warships has been governed by the Montreux Convention since 1936.
fact: The Strait of Gibraltar is only about 14 km wide at its narrowest, separating Spain in Europe from Morocco in Africa, and it is the only natural entrance to the Mediterranean from the Atlantic.
fact: The Strait of Malacca, between the Malay Peninsula and the Indonesian island of Sumatra, is one of the busiest shipping routes in the world, linking the Indian Ocean to the Pacific.
fact: Portugal seized the port of Malacca in 1511 to control the busy sea road of the spice trade there, and the Dutch took it in 1641.
fact: The Strait of Hormuz, between Iran and Oman, is the only sea exit from the Persian Gulf, and normally about a fifth of the oil the world uses passes through it.
fact: The Strait of Dover between England and France is about 33 km wide, so on a clear day you can see France from Dover, and it is one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world.
fact: The Bab-el-Mandeb, between Yemen and the Horn of Africa, is about 30 km wide and links the Red Sea to the Gulf of Aden and the Indian Ocean; ships between the Suez Canal and the Indian Ocean pass through it, and if it is blocked they must go all the way round Africa.
fact: From 1429 to 1857 Denmark charged a toll, called the Sound Dues, on every foreign ship passing through the Øresund strait between Denmark and Sweden.
fact: The Bering Strait, about 82 km wide, separates Russia from Alaska, and during the Ice Age a land bridge there let the first people walk into the Americas.
fact: Magellan found the Strait of Magellan near the tip of South America in 1520, while the wide Drake Passage, about 800 km across between Cape Horn and Antarctica, is one of the roughest seas on Earth, because strong winds and currents race round the world there with no land to stop them.
exercise: What is a strait? | A narrow stretch of sea joining two larger bodies of water | A long river that flows out of the hills and down into the sea | A wide, open stretch of ocean with no land anywhere near it | A narrow strip of land that joins two larger pieces of land
exercise: Which two seas does the Bosporus join? | The Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara | The Red Sea and the Mediterranean Sea | The North Sea and the Baltic Sea | The Caspian Sea and the Aral Sea
exercise: Which city is divided between Europe and Asia by the Bosporus? | Istanbul | Gallipoli | Thessaloniki | Alexandria
exercise: What has governed the passage of warships through the Turkish Straits since 1936? | The Montreux Convention | The Treaty of Tordesillas | The Antarctic Treaty | The Treaty of Versailles of 1919
exercise: About how wide is the Strait of Gibraltar at its narrowest? | About 14 km | About 140 km | About 1.5 km | About 1,400 km
exercise: Which two countries face each other across the Strait of Gibraltar? | Spain and Morocco | France and Algeria | Italy and Tunisia | Portugal and Mauritania
exercise: Which strait links the Indian Ocean to the Pacific between the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra? | The Strait of Malacca | The Strait of Hormuz | The Bering Strait | The Strait of Dover
exercise: Which European country seized Malacca in 1511? | Portugal | Britain | The Netherlands | France
exercise: Between which two countries is the Strait of Hormuz? | Iran and Oman | Iraq and Kuwait | Egypt and Saudi Arabia | Yemen and Djibouti
exercise: About how much of the oil the world uses normally passes through the Strait of Hormuz? | About a fifth | Almost all of it | About a hundredth | None of it
exercise: About how wide is the Strait of Dover? | About 33 km | About 3.5 km | About 330 km | About 3,300 km
exercise: Which strait links the Red Sea to the Gulf of Aden? | The Bab-el-Mandeb | The Bosporus | The Dardanelles | The Øresund
exercise: What were the Sound Dues? | A toll Denmark charged on foreign ships passing through the Øresund | A tax the King of Sweden charged on every church bell rung in his towns | Money paid by the navy to sailors who sang on long voyages | A fee Russia charged on ships crossing the Bering Strait
exercise: Which two lands does the Bering Strait separate? | Russia and Alaska | Canada and Greenland | Japan and Korea | Norway and Iceland
exercise: How did the first people reach the Americas? | They walked across a land bridge where the Bering Strait is now | They sailed across the Atlantic from Europe in Viking longships | They rowed across the Pacific from China in big wooden boats | They walked from Africa over a land bridge across the Atlantic
exercise: What is the Drake Passage? | A wide, rough sea about 800 km across between Cape Horn and Antarctica | A narrow canal cut through Panama by Francis Drake's sailors in the 1570s | A quiet river in southern England named after Francis Drake | A strait about 14 km wide between Spain and Morocco in Africa
exercise: Who found the strait near the tip of South America in 1520? | Ferdinand Magellan | Francis Drake | Christopher Columbus | Roald Amundsen
test: In Europa Universalis 5, a strait is just a crossing for armies and a good spot for a fort. What does the game leave out? | Straits differ hugely, from 14 km at Gibraltar to 800 km of rough sea at Drake | Nothing, because all real straits in the world are about the same width and depth | That real straits are only found on rivers, never between seas | That real ships can never sail through a strait, only armies walk
test: «what happens to petrol prices if ships can't get through hormuz» Which is the best answer? | Oil prices would likely jump, as about a fifth of the world's oil passes there | They would fall to almost nothing, because all the unsold oil would pile up in the Gulf | Nothing would change, because almost all oil travels by pipeline instead | Only the price of petrol in Iran would change, not anywhere else at all
test: Why did Denmark grow rich from a narrow strait? | Every foreign ship through the Øresund had to pay a toll | Denmark sold barrels of the strait's clean water to Europe | The best fish in Europe only lived in the Øresund's water | Denmark owned nearly every merchant ship in the Baltic Sea
test: Why did the Portuguese want to capture Malacca in 1511? | It controlled a narrow, busy sea route for the spice trade between two oceans | It had the biggest gold mine in Asia, worked by thousands of miners | It was the capital of the Chinese emperor and the richest and biggest city in all of China | It was the only good harbour on the whole east coast of Africa for ships
test: «the bosporus is like 50 km wide right» What is right? | It is less than 1 km wide at its narrowest | It is about 50 km wide | It is about 800 km wide | It is dry land
test: What would happen if a big ship blocked the narrow Bab-el-Mandeb? | Ships between Suez and the Indian Ocean would be held up or go round Africa | Ships could simply sail round it, as the strait there is hundreds of kilometres wide | Nothing much, because almost no ships use the Red Sea route these days | The Red Sea would slowly start to dry up without the water from the ocean
test: «why's constantinople such a big deal, it's just one city» Which is the best answer? | Whoever held it controlled all ships between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean | It had the only big church in the world, and pilgrims came to it from every country on Earth | It sat on the only gold mine in Europe, so every king wanted its money | It was the only port where ships could sail between Europe and America
test: «spain and morocco are like right next to each other innit» Is that right? | Yes, only about 14 km of sea separate them at the narrowest | No, they are on different oceans with Portugal between them | No, Africa and Europe are thousands of km apart at Gibraltar | Yes, a long road bridge has joined them at Gibraltar for years
test: «why's the sea down by cape horn so bloody rough» Which is the best answer? | Strong winds and currents race around the world there with no land to stop them | It is very shallow there, so the waves smash on the sandy bottom like a puddle | Huge icebergs break off Antarctica every day and their splashes make the waves | Underwater volcanoes keep boiling the water, so it bubbles and heaves all day
test: «can u see france from dover lol» What is right? | Yes, on a clear day, as the strait is only about 33 km wide | No, France is about 500 km away across the open North Sea | No, the curve of the Earth always hides it, even at 33 km | Yes, but only at night, when the lights of Calais are on
test: Why do Russia's warships care about the Turkish Straits? | They are the only sea way from the Black Sea to the Mediterranean | They are Russia's only way out to the Arctic Ocean and the frozen seas of the far north | They lead straight from the Black Sea to the Pacific Ocean | They are where Russia builds and repairs all its warships
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the geographical function of a strait? | A strait is a narrow passage of sea connecting two larger bodies of water | A strait is a narrow isthmus of land connecting two continents | A strait is an artificial waterway excavated by human labour | A strait is an enclosed lake without outlet
exam: Identify the states between whose coasts the Strait of Hormuz is situated. | The Islamic Republic of Iran and the Sultanate of Oman | The Republic of Iraq and the State of Kuwait | The Kingdom of Saudi Arabia and the Arab Republic of Egypt | The Republic of Turkey and the Hellenic Republic
exam: Ascertain the principal consequence of the Sound Dues levied by the Kingdom of Denmark between 1429 and 1857. | Every foreign vessel transiting the Øresund was obliged to pay a toll, a substantial source of Danish revenue | Danish vessels were prohibited from entering the Baltic Sea, which thereby passed under Swedish control | Swedish vessels were exempted from all tolls and thereby obtained a monopoly of the Baltic trade | The Øresund was closed to all foreign shipping in perpetuity, and the Baltic trade consequently ceased
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Strait of Gibraltar. | It separates Spain from Morocco, is about fourteen kilometres wide at its narrowest, and is the sole natural entrance to the Mediterranean from the Atlantic | It separates France from Algeria, measures approximately three hundred kilometres in width, and is merely one of several natural entrances to the Mediterranean Sea | It is one of several natural entrances to the Mediterranean from the Atlantic, the others lying along the coasts of Portugal and southern France | It connects the Black Sea with the Aegean Sea and is controlled under the Montreux Convention concluded between the maritime powers in 1936
